Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:08.629 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  if you've ever picked up a book and  found it's full of surreal plants  unknown diagrams zodiac rings and a  language which is completely  undecipherable you're likely looking at  a copy of the voynich manuscript the  book that nobody can translate this  medieval text which has been dated to  the early 15th century is one of the  most mysterious books ever written as  even after hundreds of years no one has  been able to definitively decode it so  for this entry into the archive we'll  take a look at this bizarre book and see  what we can learn about its history and  possible meaning the name of the  manuscript comes from wilford voynich a  polish book collector with very cool  spectacles who first discovered the book  at a college in italy in 1912. he was  understandably baffled by the bizarre  language in images within and countless  experts have been trying to make sense  of it ever since today the original copy  is stored at the beinecke rare book and  manuscript library at yale university if  you were to open the manuscript you'd  find that the first section of the  mysterious text is dedicated almost  entirely to plants most of these bizarre  flora aren't ones you'd ever find in  nature and instead seem either  completely made up or impossible hybrids  of existing plants with the roots of one  species attached to the stem of another  with the flowers of a third for good  measure only a few drawings can actually  be matched with real plant species  namely the wild pansy and a variation of  maidenhair fern but that's about all  we've got but it's not just the plant  drawings within the manuscript that are  mysterious there are also strange cosmic  wheels and astrological symbols  decorating many of the pages some of  them with stylized suns or stars these  diagrams seem related to astrology or  zodiac constellations although many of  them don't match up with familiar models  astrology did play a role in plant  gathering and medical procedures during  the period in which the manuscript was  likely written so that could be the  connection to the first section of the  book but it's hard to deduce much beyond  that aside from some pretty weird art of  people bathing in giant tubs i'm just  gonna skip over the third section of the  book has some art of what might be  alchemical equipment but as is the case  with the zodiac wheels a lot of the art  really has very little resemblance to  common alchemical equipment and symbols  of the time so what's going on here well  with most other books you just read the  words and figure out what's supposed to  be going on but that's easier said than  done with the voynich manuscript the  script is written in is completely  unknown indeed there are some theories  that that's because it's been put  through an advanced cipher or code by  the original author scrambled up to a  degree which makes it difficult to  unravel a lot of effort has been  expended to try and crack it including  some promising recent work with ai but  despite decades of trying most experts  agree the majority of the book remains  unreadable aside from a secret code it's  also possible the strange alphabet is an  attempt to write down some spoken  dialect that had no established writing  system if this is the case the alphabet  is completely unique and therefore  almost impossible to decipher another  distinct possibility however is that the  book is filled with nonsense the text  has no meaning and was designed to mess  with people but we'll come back to that  concept in a moment before we completely  give up if we can't understand the  images or the language do we at least  have some idea where it came from well  once again there are a lot of unknowns  there's a letter from the 16th century  which suggests it was once owned by holy  roman emperor rudolf ii and was then  passed on to his personal doctor jacobus  synapsis then to antique collector  george barrish then to a bohemian doctor  jan merrick marcy then perhaps to the  scholar thanos kercher then maybe to  priest peter jan bax who placed it in  the collection where voynich finally  purchased it basically the book's been  all over as for who the original author  was among all this mass some have  theorized it might have been 13th  century friar roger bacon or 15th  century italian engineer giovanni  fontana who created some surreal artwork  which does look somewhat similar others  have theorized that the whole thing is a  more modern hoax and was possibly even  fabricated by vuenich himself recent  radiocarbon dating of the parchment  however more or less rules this  possibility out as old tests have  indicated the book is almost certainly  from at least the early 15th century so  at least in that respect the manuscript  is authentic but regardless of who the  author is perhaps the most important  question is why why write this at all  it's possible that this is a genuine  medical text just written in a dialect  we can't read or it's possible it's  something even stranger but it's also  not impossible the whole purpose of this  book was to frustrate future generations  even if the manuscript isn't a modern  hoax that doesn't rule out the  possibility it's the medieval era  equivalent of a prank we sometimes think  of past societies as perpetually serious  but medieval art is often ridiculous and  seems designed to confound so it's a  distinct possibility the voynich  manuscript was made for a similar  purpose and maybe the real author is  just laughing at us from the beyond in  any case it's a fascinating and  mysterious book there's additional  research that has been done over the  years and i'd encourage you to delve in  further if you're interested and as  always thanks for watching if you  enjoyed this entry into the archive  please lend your support and like  subscribe and hit the notification icon  to stay up to date on all things curious  see you in the next video  [Music]

